### Why does the loop never terminate?

The JIT compiler is allowed to hoist invariant reads out of loops. If the variable is not marked volatile and is not accessed through a synchronized block, the JIT treats it as loop-invariant.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class InfiniteLoopBug {
    private static boolean running = true; // NOT volatile

    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        Thread worker = new Thread(() -> {
            // JIT may optimize to: if (running) while(true) { count++; }
            long count = 0;
            while (running) {
                count++;
            }
            System.out.println("Exited after " + count + " iterations");
        });

        worker.start();
        Thread.sleep(2000);
        running = false;
        System.out.println("Set running=false");
        worker.join(3000);

        if (worker.isAlive()) {
            System.out.println("BUG: worker still running after 3 seconds!");
            worker.interrupt();
        }
    }
}
```

In optimized (production) mode, the worker thread may never exit because the JIT cached `running=true` in a register.

### How does volatile fix it?

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class InfiniteLoopFix {
    private static volatile boolean running = true; // volatile!

    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        Thread worker = new Thread(() -> {
            long count = 0;
            while (running) { // volatile read: must access main memory
                count++;
            }
            System.out.println("Exited after " + count + " iterations");
        });

        worker.start();
        Thread.sleep(2000);
        running = false; // volatile write: flushes to main memory
        worker.join();
    }
}
```

```text
Exited after 847263951 iterations
```

### Why is this hard to reproduce in development?

The JIT compiler only optimizes code after it has been executed many times (the compilation threshold is typically 10,000 invocations). In debug mode or with small loop counts, the code runs in interpreted mode where every read accesses memory. The bug only manifests in production after the JIT compiles the hot loop.

You can force JIT compilation to reproduce it:

```bash
java -XX:+PrintCompilation -server InfiniteLoopBug
```

### What are the alternative fixes?

Using `synchronized` for every read also provides visibility, but it is much more expensive than volatile for a simple flag.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class SynchronizedFix {
    private static boolean running = true;
    private static final Object lock = new Object();

    public static boolean isRunning() {
        synchronized (lock) { return running; }
    }

    public static void stop() {
        synchronized (lock) { running = false; }
    }
}
```

This works because the synchronized block establishes a happens-before relationship, but acquiring a monitor lock on every loop iteration is wasteful. volatile is the right tool for this pattern.

### What does the JIT actually do?

The JIT transformation looks like this:

```text
Before JIT (interpreted, correct):
  while (running) {       // reads from memory each iteration
      work();
  }

After JIT (compiled, may hoist):
  boolean cached = running; // read ONCE, store in register
  while (cached) {          // never reads memory again!
      work();
  }
```

vollatile prevents this by inserting a memory barrier that forces the CPU to load the variable from main memory on every access.

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **x86 vs ARM**: on x86, volatile reads are cheap (just prevents register caching; the load itself is normal). On ARM, volatile reads require explicit acquire barriers, which are more expensive.
- **Thread.onSpinWait() (Java 9)**: in spin-wait loops, call `Thread.onSpinWait()` to hint the CPU. It emits a PAUSE instruction on x86, reducing power consumption and improving hyperthreading performance.
- **JMH for testing**: use JMH (Java Microbenchmark Harness) with `-XX:LoopUnrollLimit=1` to prevent JIT from unrolling loops when testing visibility bugs.
